# CROSS-CUTTING REQUIREMENTS

## A. Security and privacy

Preserve existing authentication and authorization.

Enforce tenant isolation in backend queries and tool execution.

Validate all user inputs and external URLs.

Keep credentials server-side.

Do not expose internal prompts, hidden reasoning, private business information, API keys, or other tenants' data.

Treat all externally retrieved text as untrusted input.

## B. Sales quality

Preserve Alpha's role as a consultative AI Sales Manager.

It must:

- Understand visitor intent before pitching.
- Maintain relevant conversational context.
- Respect hypothetical scenarios and corrections.
- Answer direct questions before asking unnecessary follow-ups.
- Use tenant-specific verified facts for business claims.
- Apply ethical persuasion and handle objections naturally.
- Avoid repetitive sales pitches in casual conversation.
- Offer only conversion actions the business actually supports.
- Avoid false scarcity, fake testimonials, fabricated results, and unsupported guarantees.

Do not turn web research into an excuse to interrupt every conversation with irrelevant market information.

## C. Error handling and observability

Implement useful, privacy-conscious logging and monitoring.

Distinguish model errors, retrieval errors, search failures, rate limits, validation failures, timeouts, and rendering problems.

Provide bounded retries and graceful fallbacks.

Never hide errors by reporting success when the backend operation failed.

## D. Backward compatibility

Preserve existing customers, tenants, widget identifiers, Company Brain data, conversations, and working integrations.

Use backward-compatible schema changes or controlled migrations.

Back up or otherwise protect existing data before destructive changes.

Do not reset production data or replace working configuration with sample values.

